## PR Title
Use ORCID API to get info for ORCID authors

## PR Description
**Description**
This PR uses the ORCID API to get information for ORCID authors defined in the .dockstore.yml.

Notes:
- Database schema changes to add the name, email, affiliation, and role columns to the orcidauthor table. Did this by creating an AbstractAuthor class which the non-ORCID author and ORCID author classes extend from.
- Removed the `CascadeType.ALL` annotation from Version.orcidAuthors. Thinking about it now, it doesn't really make sense since an OrcidAuthor can belong to multiple versions. If one version is deleted, you don't want to delete the OrcidAuthor from the table because it might be referred to by another version. 
  - Right now, I don't handle the deletion of OrcidAuthors if they're not linked to any versions. Is it okay to keep them around, or should they be deleted?
- A read-public access token for ORCID is requested if the user that initiates the GitHub release doesn't have an ORCID token. According to [ORCID](https://info.orcid.org/documentation/api-tutorials/api-tutorial-read-data-on-a-record/#easy-faq-2532), this token is long-lived and can last 20 years. Is there somewhere we can save this token? Or is it okay to request one every time a user doesn't have their own token?
  - A similar case where an access token is requested and not saved in our DB is for the [DockerRegistryApiHelper](https://github.com/dockstore/dockstore/blob/23b4caddd4fe6b6924ff653aef3f159123116848/dockstore-webservice/src/main/java/io/dockstore/webservice/helpers/DockerRegistryAPIHelper.java#L214), so maybe it's okay that we don't save the ORCID access token.
  - We could alternatively assume that the user will have an ORCID token, but I can imagine scenarios where the user who initiates the GitHub release won't have an ORCID token.
- Author information for existing ORCID authors is updated every GitHub release.
- Rewrote the existing test in `WebhookIT` to use Hoverly for the ORCID API calls. Chose to do this because the ORCID IDs in https://github.com/DockstoreTestUser2/test-authors don't exist on the sandbox environment and I didn't want to rewrite the ORCID IDs in the repo to existing ones. Let me know if it's preferred to use the ORCID sandbox environment instead of mocking it with Hoverfly.
  - I needed to use the LocalTrustStore in order to get this test to pass, so I changed the CircleCI config.yml to use our trust store for all tests.
- Added a `workflows/{workflowId}/workflowVersions/{workflowVersionId}` endpoint so that the UI can use this endpoint to get authors. This fixes the null OrcidAuthor problems in the UI. View the corresponding frontend PR for more details: https://github.com/dockstore/dockstore-ui2/pull/1475

**Issue**
[SEAB-3220](https://ucsc-cgl.atlassian.net/browse/SEAB-3220)

