## PR Title
SMS submission

## PR Description
Closes #242

This PR is a WIP of the whole feature implementation. It contains the components that are required for sending a form as an SMS to a destination. Below is a diagram of the architecture that was designed.

![sms architecture 2](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/1509205/39378120-f071d3b8-4a46-11e8-8f9f-9db8e6c473db.png)

The architecture revolves around the [SMS Service](https://github.com/jd-alexander/collect/blob/sms_submission/collect_app/src/main/java/org/odk/collect/android/tasks/sms/SmsService.java) that acts as the main component with the responsibility for sending forms and dispatching events to the UI. 

This design was chosen for due to the nature of how SMS functionality works on devices and how form submissions should be handled based on the discussions on the [ODK forum](https://forum.opendatakit.org/t/send-submissions-via-sms/12166/34). Some points that stood out from the discussion that inspired this approach are : 

- The case for proper background sending due to the number of messages that need to be sent when multiple form submissions are in place. [details](https://forum.opendatakit.org/t/send-submissions-via-sms/12166/32)
- The ability to track each message to ensure that it's sent and no data is lost. [details](https://forum.opendatakit.org/t/send-submissions-via-sms/12166/24)
-  Network outages, insufficient funds for sending SMS & dead batteries. 
- Sending one message at a time so that submissions can be cancelled, and messages are only be sent if there's success with the previous one.

When a form upload is triggered the first thing that takes place is it's persistence to storage.
The [SMS Submission Manager](https://github.com/jd-alexander/collect/blob/sms_submission/collect_app/src/main/java/org/odk/collect/android/tasks/sms/SmsSubmissionManagerImpl.java) is a persistence layer powered by Shared Preferences that takes the divided message from the SMS Manager and creates a submission so that the messages and their state can be tracked. This tracking allows the app to remember the progress of a form upload after restarts or when the app closes. This eliminates possibility for loss of data and ensures that the events for the UI that can be informative such as the progress of the submission.

Once the messages have been saved [a background job](https://github.com/jd-alexander/collect/blob/sms_submission/collect_app/src/main/java/org/odk/collect/android/tasks/sms/SmsSenderJob.java) to send the message is then created and the job is added to the queue. Background jobs were utilized due to dynamic behavior that's expected when it comes unto submissions. There are several different constraints that are necessary to send a job and most importantly to retry when there's failure. To accomplish this task I opted for a [Job Queue component](https://github.com/jd-alexander/android-priority-jobqueue) which I forked, updated to simplify its dependencies. Using a job queue allows us to specify those constraints and ensure that a text is only sent when necessary. This also allows us to save resources because a message won't be resent if it has failed due to a lack of network. A `NetworkListener` could be plugged into the job queue with ease to only fire a message when the device has regained signal, the same can be done if the device was in airplane mode. Another important point is the execution of jobs can be controlled as the job manager can be instructed to only execute `x` amount of jobs so that the app isn't flooded with too many events and failures can be handled more gracefully.  

Once the background job sends a message, the [SentBroadcastReceiver](https://github.com/jd-alexander/collect/blob/sms_submission/collect_app/src/main/java/org/odk/collect/android/tasks/sms/SentBroadcastReceiver.java) is triggered when it was successfully sent. Each message is tagged with a unique id that identifies each `PendingIntent` so each message can be marked as sending/sent and the system can react likewise. 

The results from the `BroadcastReceiver` are then processed by the `SMSService` so that the app can react based on the result. Some of the reactions are : 

- If a message was sent, then check to see if there's another message that needs to be sent, add it to the queue. (Implemented). 
- If the device currently has no signal then the `JobManager` can delay the job and attach a network listener so that the submission can continue later ( Not Yet Implemented)
- If there's a generic failure of a message then the user is updated via the UI and the submission is halted until the user triggers it again. (Not Yet Implemented) 

